===Noun===
'''introvert'''
* One who is energized by being alone and whose energy is drained by being around other people.  An introvert derives mental and emotional energy from their internal world (emotions and ideas), while an extrovert draws from the outside world (people and activities).  <ref>[http://cbae.nmsu.edu/%7Edboje/teaching/490_psl/step_2.htm "Myers Briggs, XYZ Leadership, and Team Roles" by David M. Boje, Ph.D.  January 21, 2001, David Boje, Inside the Leadership Box]</ref> Introversion is often mistakenly conflated with shyness, moodiness or selfcenteredness, and should be distinguished from these concepts. <ref>[http://www.youthspecialties.com/articles/topics/relational/shy.php Am I Too Shy? Relational Youth Work for Introverts, Jenny Baker]</ref>

* According to Carl Jung, introversion and [[extrovert|
extroversion]] are reflections of the preferred orientation (within themselves or outside of themselves) of a person's attention and source of emotional sustenance (something Jung called 'psychic energy' -- a term considered antiquated by Modern Jungians). An introvert's orientation is internal.  When percieving an object or situation, an introvert will react by examining the relationship between themselves and the object or situation.  In contrast, and extrovert will direct their energy onto the object or situation and away from themselves. <ref> [http://www.time.com/time/magazine/article/0,9171,807036-3,00.html The Old Wise Man] Time magazine article about Jung, Feb. 14, 1955 </ref> 

